To make Bootstrap tables sortable alphanumerically without using jQuery or other plugins, you can use the HTML5 Drag and Drop API. Here’s a step-by-step guide:

1. **HTML Structure**: Ensure your table has a structure that supports sorting. Each header cell (`<th>`) should have a unique identifier or class to distinguish it.

2. **CSS Styling**: Add some basic CSS to style the table and the headers. For example, you can add a class to the headers to indicate they are sortable.

3. **JavaScript Implementation**: Use the HTML5 Drag and Drop API to handle the sorting logic. Here’s a basic example:

```html
<table class="table" id="sortableTable">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th draggable="true" class="sortable">Name</th>
      <th draggable="true" class="sortable">Age</th>
      <th draggable="true" class="sortable">City</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>John</td>
      <td>25</td>
      <td>New York</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Jane</td>
      <td>30</td>
      <td>Los Angeles</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<script>
  const table = document.getElementById('sortableTable');
  const headers = table.querySelectorAll('th.sortable');

  headers.forEach(header => {
    header.addEventListener('dragstart', (e) => {
      e.dataTransfer.setData('text/plain', header.id);
    });
  });

  table.addEventListener('dragover', (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();
  });

  table.addEventListener('drop', (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    const data = e.dataTransfer.getData('text/plain');
    const draggedHeader = document.querySelector(`th[id="${data}"]`);
    const targetHeader = e.target.closest('th');

    if (draggedHeader && targetHeader) {
      const headersArray = Array.from(headers);
      const draggedIndex = headersArray.indexOf(draggedHeader);
      const targetIndex = headersArray.indexOf(targetHeader);

      if (draggedIndex !== targetIndex) {
        headersArray.splice(draggedIndex, 1);
        headersArray.splice(targetIndex, 0, draggedHeader);
        headersArray.forEach((header, index) => {
          header.style.order = index;
        });
      }
    }
  });
</script>
```

This code sets up a basic sortable table where you can drag and drop headers to reorder the columns. The `dragstart` event captures the header being dragged, and the `drop` event reorders the headers based on the drop location.

What is the purpose of the `dragstart` event in the JavaScript code?

The `dragstart` event is triggered when the user starts dragging an element. In this context, it is used to store the ID of the header being dragged in the data transfer object. This allows the `drop` event to identify which header was dragged and where it should be placed.

How does the `drop` event work in the JavaScript code?

The `drop` event is triggered when the user drops the dragged element onto a valid drop target. In this case, it is used to reorder the table headers based on the drop location. The event handler retrieves the ID of the dragged header from the data transfer object, finds the target header where the drop occurred, and then reorders the headers in the DOM accordingly.

