The content script and the popup script are running in different contexts, so they cannot directly access each other's variables. However, you can use the chrome.runtime.sendMessage API to communicate between them.

In your popup.js, you can send a message to the content script using the following code:

chrome.runtime.sendMessage("content_script", { data: "your data" });

In your content script, you can listen for messages from the popup script using the following code:

chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((message, sender, sendResponse) => {
if (sender.id === "popup_script") {
// Handle the message from the popup script
console.log(message.data);
}
});

This way, the content script can receive data from the popup script and use it as needed.

Note that you should also handle the case where the popup script is not running or has not sent a message yet. You can do this by checking if the message is defined before using it.

Also, make sure to handle the case where the content script is not running or has not received a message yet. You can do this by checking if the message is defined before using it.

Another option is to use a shared storage mechanism, such as chrome.storage.local, to store the data and access it from both the popup script and the content script. This way, you don't have to rely on the popup script to send the data to the content script.

In your popup.js, you can save the data to chrome.storage.local using the following code:

chrome.storage.local.set({ data: "your data" });

In your content script, you can retrieve the data from chrome.storage.local using the following code:

chrome.storage.local.get("data", (result) => {
// Handle the data
console.log(result.data);
});

This way, both the popup script and the content script can access the data stored in chrome.storage.local.

