```java
public class QuickSort {
    public static void quickSort(int[] arr) {
        if (arr == null || arr.length <= 1) return;
        quickSort(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);
    }

    private static void quickSort(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
        if (low < high) {
            // Partition the array and get the pivot index
            int pi = partition(arr, low, high);

            // Recursively sort elements before and after partition
            quickSort(arr, low, pi - 1);
            quickSort(arr, pi + 1, high);
        }
    }

    private static int partition(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
        // Pick the last element as pivot
        int pivot = arr[high];
        int i = (low - 1); // Index of smaller element

        for (int j = low; j < high; j++) {
            // If current element is smaller than or equal to pivot
            if (arr[j] <= pivot) {
                i++;
                // Swap arr[i] and arr[j]
                int temp = arr[i];
                arr[i] = arr[j];
                arr[j] = temp;
            }
        }
        // Swap arr[i+1] and arr[high] (put pivot in correct place)
        int temp = arr[i + 1];
        arr[i + 1] = arr[high];
        arr[high] = temp;
        return (i + 1);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {25, 2, 8, 60, 3, 1, 90};
        quickSort(arr);

        for (int i : arr) {
            System.out.print(i + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

**Why this is better:**
1.  **Correctness:** The original Hoare partition logic (`while(arr[i] < pivot)`) fails if `arr[i] == pivot`. This version uses `<=` and handles duplicates safely.
2.  **Readability:** Separating the `partition` logic into its own method makes the code easier to debug and maintain.
3.  **Simplicity:** It removes the complex `i <= j` swapping logic which is prone to off-by-one errors.

